"""Curated authorial questions; code only formats already-read occurrences."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
OUT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
claims={int(c['id'].split('-')[-1])-45000:c for c in json.loads((OUT/'claims.json').read_text())}
P=OUT.parents[2]/'sources/SA18_texts/pro/MPL_45_033-080_transcription.tex'
C=OUT.parents[2]/'sources/SA18_texts/codex'
problems=[]
def p(n,title,cs,as_stated,today,art,worth,why=None,extra=(),refs=None):
    occurrences=[]
    for i in cs:
        c=claims[i]
        occurrences.append({'claim':c['id'],'cote':c['cote'],'scans':c['scans'],'lines':c['lines']})
    occurrences.extend(extra)
    rr=refs if refs is not None else sorted({r for i in cs for r in claims[i]['refs']})
    problems.append(dict(id=f'MP-SA18-{45000+n}',title=title,occurrences=occurrences,as_he_states=as_stated,plausible_today=today,state_of_art='As of19November2026: '+art,why_open=why or 'The cited manuscript passage leaves this step open or qualified. No independent historical source was found that explains why the author stopped here; no motive is inferred.',worth=worth,refs=rr))
p(1,'Recovering integral functions from distributions',[5],'Proposition2 is to follow from the completion square; A-connectedness is inserted and the proof stops at separated completion.','For a precisely defined smooth/connected affine group overA, characterize whichB-functions with integral identity-distribution pairings descend toA; distinguish the formal neighbourhood from global functions.','SGA3 VIIB §§1–2 establishes formal-group/hyperalgebra duality. It does not settle this exact global statement without the manuscript’s A-simple/A-connected conventions.','Worth a short textual and hypothesis audit before attempting any theorem: it would identify whether the remaining issue is global descent or merely notation.')
p(2,'Smoothness of Lie automorphism schemes',[9],'«sauf erreur»: H²(g,g)=0 should makeAut(g) smooth, with surrounding Artinian-base hypotheses.','For a finite free Lie algebra, a square-zero bracket-defect obstruction lies inH²(g,g⊗I); its vanishing gives a lift of an automorphism once the coefficient/base-change assumptions are fixed.','Nijenhuis–Richardson deformation theory proves the characteristic0 field version; the elementary square-zero calculation is written inCL45009. A theorem over every Artinian base requires the relative cohomology condition, not just vanishing on one fibre.','Worth checking the exact relative hypothesis and the ink; a few hours could produce a self-contained corrected lemma.',refs=['AG45-NR1966','AG45-NR1967'])
p(3,'Obstruction degrees and rigidity',[10,11],'H² should imply rigidity; H³ should remove lifting obstructions, with uniqueness conditions nearby.','Lie deformations are controlled by the shifted Chevalley–Eilenberg complex: degree2 classes parameterize first-order changes and degree3 classes obstruct extension.','Nijenhuis–Richardson’s graded-Lie deformation formalism is the exact developed subject. H³ vanishing alone does not imply uniqueness.','Low priority as new research; useful for an accurate commentary on the hierarchy of claims.',refs=['AG45-NR1966','AG45-NR1967'])
p(4,'Unspecified group-to-Lie spectral sequence',[12],'A cohomology comparison and a «suite spectrale» are suggested, without identifying the cohomology theory.','Specify rational algebraic-group cohomology, infinitesimal/restricted-Lie cohomology, or cohomology of a classifying topos before formulating a comparison.','SGA3 VIIA treats infinitesimal groups; CCMT §3.7 gives classifying-topos spectral sequences. These are different constructions and do not identify the manuscript’s single intended sequence.','Worth a limited reconstruction of the candidate meanings, not a claim that a new spectral sequence remains open.')
p(5,'Adjoint cohomology in positive characteristic',[13],'The semisimple adjoint-cohomology vanishing is queried without a secure characteristic restriction.','Retain characteristic0 Whitehead vanishing; classify modular exceptions for the specified root data and cohomology theory.','The unrestricted version fails: pgl₂/F₂ has3-dimensionalH¹, computed exactly here. Published reductive-group theory retains the small-characteristic distinctions.','Worth an explicit small-rank appendix; one run can give reproducible counterexamples and prevent a false general theorem.')
p(6,'Chevalley groups from Lie rigidity',[18],'The margin suggests constructing integral Chevalley groups from vanishing/rigidity of their Lie algebras.','Construction of split reductiveZ-groups requires integral root data and root subgroups, with bad-prime Lie phenomena treated separately.','SGA3 and Conrad’s construction from pinned root data are established; the exact Lie-only route is not justified by the modular vanishing proposed on these pages.','Worth documenting the obstruction to this route, with no claim that Chevalley existence itself is open.')
p(7,'Lifting outer automorphisms of inner forms',[22,32,35],'Is every outer automorphism lifted? The later table recordsNon and proposes real unitary examples.','Lifting requires the outer automorphism to stabilize the relevant inner twisting class; the pinned split/quasi-split situation must be separated.','Conrad §7.2, especially Example7.2.10 and Proposition7.2.12, gives the precise real-unitary and quasi-split context. The manuscript already withdraws the blanket claim.','Worth matching the exact unitary example to the ink; no new universal lifting theorem should be pursued.')
p(8,'Conjugacy of Borels over local bases',[24,36],'Are two Borels always conjugate, in particular whenS is local? The last table hasinjectif/bijectif uncertain.','For a reductive group scheme, Borels are conjugate étale locally. Rational or base-ring conjugacy requires the appropriate local hypotheses and torsor fibre; surjectivity ofH¹(B)→H¹(G) is a separate assertion.','Conrad Theorem5.2.11 establishes the Borel scheme and étale-local conjugacy; its corollaries give the split and henselian cases. They do not give the manuscript’s possible arbitrary-local global assertion.','Worth a precise catalogue of the local hypotheses and the injective/bijective reading; a few hours can resolve the intended standard case.')
p(9,'Existence and conjugacy of maximal tori',[25,37],'The boxed local question concerns maximal tori; the later table says general Cartan conjugacy is false even over fields.','For reductiveG/S the maximal-torus scheme is smooth surjective; global tori and rational conjugacy must be distinguished from étale-local existence and conjugacy.','Conrad Theorem3.2.6 and Corollary3.2.7 provide the scheme and local theorem. Split and anisotropic real tori are already a rational-conjugacy obstruction.','Worth recording the precise failed global formulation, not pursuing the false universal assertion.')
p(10,'Normalizer splitting and compatible Killing pairs',[26,27],'The margin proposesN₁=T₁⋊W₁ and uses it to lift Weyl torsors; a compatible-pair conjugacy question follows.','Specify whetherN₁ belongs toG₁ orits adjoint form and whether splitting is an assumption. Compatible(T,B) pairs are governed by their stabilizer, not by the Weyl group alone.','SGA3/Conrad gives normalizers and Weyl representatives. InSL₂overcharacteristic≠2 a nontrivial Weyl representative has square−I, refuting an unrestricted splitting. The adjoint reading remains to be checked.','High editorial value: resolving one overwritten group label determines whether the claimed argument has a counterexample.')
p(11,'Reconstructing a Borel pair from its torus',[28],'The equivalence theorem and its proof are cancelled, with «J’en doute» nearby.','Reconstruction needs a based root datum and compatible descent action in addition to the abstract torus.','SGA3 XXIV–XXV classifies pinned forms with precisely this extra structure. The corrected category is established; the torus-only category is insufficient.','Worth a short comparison of the cancelled category and the published root-datum category.')
p(12,'Tsen and imperfect-field vanishing',[31,89,97,98],'Tsen-type andcd≤1 hypotheses are used to predict torsor lifting, including possibly nonperfect fields.','Over perfectcd≤1 fields use Steinberg and Grothendieck–Springer. For imperfect fields specify flat coefficients and Brauer vanishing over the required extensions, rather than ordinarycd_p alone.','Flicker–Scheiderer–Sujatha Introduction and§3 state the perfect-field theorem and clarify nonabelianH²; Harari §2.4 explains the characteristicp caveat.','Worth isolating the exact flat-cohomological version of the manuscript, with its hypotheses, before claiming a new theorem.')
p(13,'Reducing Borel torsors to torus torsors',[33],'TheB→T reduction is left to verify.','ForB=T⋊U over an affine base with a suitable split vector-group filtration ofU and its twists, H¹(U)=0 gives reduction toT.','Conrad’s proof of Corollary5.2.13 uses this filtration argument. It does not cover arbitrary wound unipotent groups or arbitrary nonaffine bases.','Low-cost useful completion of the commentary under explicit split/affine hypotheses.')
p(14,'The uncertain rank-one Weyl example',[34],'A Weyl-orbit argument is sketched for a group readGP(2)orGL(2).','Compute the orbit mapH¹(T)→H¹(G) only after fixing the ambient group, central quotient and the meaning ofmodD/modB.','The nonabelian twisting framework is established in Conrad §7.2; the manuscript does not yet identify a unique worked example.','Worth one scan-led run; without the group label, developing the example would manufacture missing text.')
p(15,'Quadratic times quadratic in characteristic2',[47],'The tensor table ends in question marks about a quadratic×quadratic product.','A bilinear form can act on a quadratic form. There is no natural symmetric quadratic×quadratic operation satisfying the indicated pure-tensor and symmetry requirements in characteristic2.','EKM §8 describes the valid bilinear action. The exhaustiveF₂⁴ calculation here leaves two possible refinements interchanged by factor swap, hence no symmetric choice in the tested universal case.','Worth a concise no-go statement with explicit axioms; the experiment is already exact and can support a short universal proof.')
p(16,'The Sigma, symmetric and Frobenius diagrams',[46],'The diagrams and tensor table are repeatedly revised, withΣ²/Σ₂and dual symbols uncertain.','State separately symmetric tensors, symmetric quotient, alternating forms and the Frobenius-twisted diagonal map, for a finite locally free module in characteristic2.','EKM §§8–10 distinguishes bilinear and quadratic objects. No exact sequence can be assigned to the damaged diagram until its targets are read correctly.','High textual value: a rank-and-functor audit can eliminate impossible readings, while the scan remains decisive.')
p(17,'A characteristic-independent quadratic invariant theory',[54,129,130,131,132],'A cohomological theory valid in every characteristic is requested; the letter already distinguishesμ₂andZ/2.','UseflatKummer coefficientsμ₂for units andBrauer2-torsion, étaleArtin–Schreier coefficientsZ/2for characteristic2 quadratic extensions, and the appropriate parity-dependent orthogonal group schemes.','Stacks59.28.3 and59.63.1 give the two exact sequences; Conrad AppendixC confirms the parity distinction. A single undifferentiatedétaleZ/2 theory is not the author’s final position.','Worth an organized comparison of coefficients and low-degree invariants, with the letter’s correction preserved.')
p(18,'Henselian valuation formulas without discreteness',[59,64],'The worddiscrètementis deleted in a valued-field example; a laterJordan endpoint is queried.','For henselian valuations with residue characteristic≠2, keep the square classes of the value group as well as residue-field Witt data. The familiar two-copy formula needs a discrete rank-one valuation and a uniformizer.','EKM §19 treatsDVR residue and specialization; its hypotheses do not support an arbitrary-value-group two-summand formula.','Worth determining the exact field class and filtration notation before extending theDVR theorem.')
p(19,'The Hasse target and torsion subgroup',[60,61],'Hasse local-global maps are drawn withΣ; a signature-zero torsion assertion is made.','For a number field use the correctly restricted product/product of local invariants; distinguish rank, determinant, Hasse invariants and real signatures.','Classical quadratic-form local-global theory and EKM’s signature/torsion chapters give the established result. A literal direct sum of all square-class components is too small for every global form.','Worth a normalization audit of the displayed map; the underlying Hasse theorem is settled.')
p(20,'The Picard-over-two term over a Dedekind ring',[62,63],'A diagram placesPic(A)/2Pic(A) besideF′(A), with equality/isomorphism strokes unclear.','FlatKummer givesPic(A)/2→H²(A,μ₂). Identifying this with a quadratic invariant requires the definition ofF′, a nonsingularity convention and a precise comparison map.','Stacks59.28.3 proves the Kummer term. Neither the letter nor the reused arithmetic diagram identifiesF′well enough to claim the stronger equality.','Worth recoveringF′from adjacent scans and comparing it to the Kummer image; this is a bounded editorial problem.')
p(21,'Injectivity of the degree-two quadratic invariant',[121],'Letter22July1961 asks whethergrad²Q(k)→H²(k,Z/2) is always injective.','For a fieldk ofcharacteristic≠2, identify the rank-zeroGrothendieck–Witt filtration with the Witt fundamental filtration; thenI²/I³→H²(k,Z/2) is an isomorphism.','The degree2norm-residue theorem ofMerkurjev settles the cohomological map; EKM ChapterVIII gives an opened modern proof. TheGW-toW filtration identification and the degree2 theorem are separate steps, as parent’sDelzant review also verifies.','Historically important and fully solved; worth an exact1961-to1981 commentary, not a new research claim.',why='The letter explicitly asks for injectivity. Later sources prove it, but do not explain why the author did not pursue this draft further.')
p(22,'Exterior operations and the augmentation filtration',[124],'The letter specifiesλ_t onlineforms and asks for theλ-structure/filtration to be made explicit.','Exterior powers give a specialλ-ring structure onGW; for connected semilocal rings with2invertible, theγ-filtration equals augmentation powers and induces powersI onW.','Zibrowius Theorem2.5 and Proposition4.1 prove these precise assertions; higher filtration comparisons on general schemes have extra conditions and can be strict.','Worth a precise comparison ofλ,γandaugmentation terminology; the local-ring formulation is established.',refs=['AG45-Zibrowius2018','AG45-EKM2008'])
p(23,'Universal total Stiefel–Whitney target',[122],'The total class is proposed as a universal homomorphism toZ×(1+H⁺), with multiplication not fully fixed.','Construct total classes from line elements and Whitney sums; a universal ring object additionally requires explicit multiplication and completion conventions.','EKM §5 supplies the field total class; CCMT §§4–5 supplies universal classifying-topos classes. These sources do not identify an undefined product on the manuscript’s target set.','Worth reconstructing the target operations from the letter andDelzant’s printed version, with normalization checked first.')
p(24,'Replacing étale cohomology by pi-one cohomology',[125],'Everything should extend to a local ring ofresiduecharacteristic≠2, usingcohomologyofπ₁.','Diagonalisation extends locally when2isinvertible; identificationH*(π₁,−)=H*_ét requires aK(π,1)condition and cannot be assumed for every local scheme.','CCMT’s scheme theory uses the étale classifying topos. It gives characteristic classes without the manuscript’s unqualifiedπ₁replacement.','Worth finding the intended local-ring class; the important correction is cohomological, not merely notational.')
p(25,'Stiefel–Whitney classes on schemes',[126],'The letter proposes characteristic classes of quadratic modules on arbitrary schemes with2invertible.','For a unimodular symmetric bundle over a scheme with2invertible, étaleHasse–Witt classes are functorial and satisfy the established splitting/Whitney properties.','EKV1993 and Jardine’s work, compared in CCMT2013 §§4–5, develop this theory. CCMT Theorem1.1 gives exact determinant/Clifford correction terms rather than a convention-freeall-rankw₂identity.','Worth a historical and normalization commentary; the existence question itself is settled.')
p(26,'Comparison with topological characteristic classes',[127],'The complex comparison should follow by a rank1 test andGAGA.','Under the complex étale/classical cohomology comparison, algebraic orthogonal-bundle characteristic classes match the corresponding topological ones. A local-ring limit andπ₁comparison are additional assertions.','EKV’s class construction and CCMT’s universal comparison supply the modern setting; the sentence invokingGAGA alone does not prove every map written in the letter.','Worth checking the exact comparison diagram and separating it from theπ₁question.')
p(27,'Serre’s proposed pro-two refinement',[123],'The letter reports a suggestion to use a maximalpro2extension and obtain a finerH².','Specify the actual Galois quotient or extension closure, then examine inflation to absoluteGalois cohomology; symbols generated byH¹provide constraints but do not define a strict refinement.','EKM’s norm-residue results settle the symbol cohomology of fields; Harari gives the Galois-cohomological framework. The short reported suggestion has no uniquely identifiable stronger theorem.','Worth recovering the exact extension definition from the scan or correspondence before any computation.')
p(28,'Degenerate forms and mixed coefficients',[128,129,130,131],'The last letter sheet proposesQ′=Q+Z fordegenerate forms and the characteristic2Kummer/Artin–Schreier invariants.','Over fields with2invertible, radical splitting gives the statedrank-retainingproduct. Characteristic2 requires separate treatment of the polar radical and square terms.','The radical-splitting calculation is elementary; EKM’s characteristic2 sections and the two Stacks exact sequences give the precise modern distinctions.','Useful as a short explanatory appendix; do not infer a characteristic2quadratic ring from the characteristic≠2formula.')
p(29,'Deferred exterior-contraction identity',[82,83,84,85],'Equation34 is deferred to§6n°4; the subsequent pairing is expressly said not to determine contraction uniquely.','For finite projective modules the perfectpairing proves the shuffle/contraction identities; for arbitrary modules define the contraction by coproduct before testing the pairing.','Bourbaki’s exterior algebra is the reused source family. The coproduct/shuffle calculation inCL45080–45088 directly checks the formulas, including the nonuniqueness warning for torsion modules.','Worth a sign-and-index appendix, primarily to guide the scan checks.')
p(30,'Lifting torsors from commutative H-two vanishing',[89,90,93,94],'Lemmas1–4 try to reduce a general epimorphism tocommutative kernels, smooth parts andradicial parts.','Keep the perfect-fieldGrothendieck–Springer theorem separate from an imperfect-field flat-topology extension; all twists and kernel descents must satisfy thevanishing hypothesis.','Flicker–Scheiderer–Sujatha Introduction and§3 give the developed perfect-field result. The proof on these pages has an invalidCartan-normalizer step and ambiguoussmooth-reduction hypotheses.','High value for a proof audit: locate exactly which extra hypothesis repairs the reduction without claiming the theorem false from a failed proof.')
p(31,'The Cartan-normalizer reduction',[95],'Conjugacy ofCartan subalgebras is used to concludeN_G(h)K=G.','This equality needs more than infinitesimalCartan conjugacy; distinguish conjugacy underGfrom conjugacy under an infinitesimal normal subgroupK.','ForSL₂overan algebraicallyclosedfieldofcharacteristicp>2,K=kerF,h=LieT, the equalityfails becauseKhastrivialreducedpart. SGA3 VIIA §7 explains theheight-one kernel.','Worth an explicit correction/counterexample note; this is a precise failure in the proof, not automatically a refutation ofLemma4.')
p(32,'Finite flat coefficients and the alpha-p boundary',[96,97],'The page writesH^i(k,α_p)=0forall iand reduces finitecommutative coefficients toBrauer vanishing.','Correcttheα_pstatementtoi≥2, unlesskisperfect. Require the appropriate Brauer conditions for μ_pand othermultiplicative-type constituents.','FlatFrobenius exactness givesH¹(k,α_p)=k/k^p; higher groupsvanish. Stacks quasi-coherent flatcohomology andHarari’s warning aboutimperfectcd_pgive thecorrect boundary.','Worth checking one quantifier on the scan; the mathematical correction is explicit.')
p(33,'Neutrality of nonabelian H-two',[99],'An algebraic kernel should have one neutralH²class under thevanishing hypotheses; the proof uses induction.','Over perfect fields ofcohomologicaldimension≤1, theGrothendieck–Springer result holds in its continuousalgebraickernel formulation. Otherbase/topology cases need their own hypotheses.','Flicker–Scheiderer–Sujatha §1 repairs acontinuity issueinSpringer’s formulation and§3givesthetheorem. Perfectnessdoesnotimplycentrelessness, so the manuscript’s induction cannot use that implication.','Historically useful for comparing the draft with the published kernel formalism and its corrections.')
p(34,'Realising bands by group sheaves',[100],'Can a kernel on a site be represented by a group sheaf? TheI/Zandnormalizerquotients are uncertain.','Specify the band and its outer action; existence of a representing group object and neutralisation of itsgerbe are distinct obstruction problems.','Giraud’s nonabeliancohomology andCCMT §3.5give the exact sequence formalism. The damaged quotient label prevents identifying one unique obstruction in the manuscript.','Worth recovering the notation and testing a small site; developing a new general theory is premature.')
p(35,'Descent of the geometric radical',[102,108],'A characteristic2matrix example is meant to show that the radical is notdefinedoverk; root criteria are latercancelled.','The scheme-theoreticcentre isdefinedoverk, while its geometricallyreduced connectedpart can fail to descend over animperfectfield.','The displayedideal directly verifies this distinction; modern algebraic-group theory separates central subgroup schemes from smooth geometricradicals. The root-weight criterion waswithdrawn.','High explanatory value: one short explicitexample clarifies why the wordscentreandradicalcannot be interchanged.')
p(36,'Embedding flag varieties by their Lie algebras',[103,104,106,109,110],'The Grassmannian map should be aclosedimmersion if the Lie normalizer is theBorel; severalrank criteria are cancelled.','For theproperflag scheme, require geometricinjectivity andinjective tangentmap. The latter isLieN_G(LieB)=LieB andfails inbadcharacteristic.','SGA3 XXII andConrad §5.2establishflag schemes; thesl₂characteristic2calculation gives a concretefailure of this particularGrassmannian immersion.','Worth an exact small-characteristic classification for the root data on the page; a few hours can settle rankone completely.')
